.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1248 AM PDT Thu Aug 6 2026
(Today and tonight)

Longwave upper-level ridging will drift in from the Nevada and the
Desert SW today, compressing the marine layer to near 1,500 feet
in the process. Temperatures will be near to slightly above normal
with the diurnal sea breeze. A surge of monsoonal moisture
arrives today, increasing surface dew points and relative humidity
in the process. Sensible weather wise, this will make it feel
hotter and more uncomfortable than what the thermometer reads.
Areas of moderate HeatRisk are expected in the interior with
pockets of major in the higher terrain of Monterey and San Benito
Counties. Stagnant air from the ridge may support haze from
wildfire smoke. Elevated fire weather conditions continue today
above the marine layer.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1248 AM PDT Thu Aug 6 2026
(Friday through Wednesday)

Surface high pressure over the Four Corners will build and expand
westward Friday and into the weekend. This will bring the hottest
conditions of the week and compress the marine layer further. Areas
of moderate HeatRisk across the interior on Friday will expand to
become widespread on Saturday with areas of major. To put this into
context: 850mb temperatures, 500mb heights, and PWAT values are all
forecast to exceed the 90th percentile Saturday. No records are
forecast with temperatures 10-15 degrees above normal for the
hottest spots. A potential wild card in the precision and subsequent
impacts of the forecast is the aforementioned surge of monsoonal
moisture. Mid-to-high level clouds would limit solar insolation
during the day and radiational cooling at night, potentially
resulting in cooler highs and warmer lows than the official
forecast. Even though the ridge will begin to break down and dry air
will begin to get filtered in from the northwest, cumulative impacts
from the heat and humidity will likely still be felt on Sunday. Near-
critical fire weather conditions are expected above the compressed
marine layer Friday through the weekend due to hot and dry
conditions with the diurnal sea breeze. Global ensemble clusters are
in agreement that upper-level shortwave troughing off the West Coast
will prevent the aforementioned ridge and subtropical high pressure
across the Eastern Pacific Ocean from joining forces and building
out of control across our area. Sensible weather wise, temperatures
will moderate to near normal with onshore flow Monday through
Wednesday. Minor coastal flooding is expected during the highest
high tide (evening/overnight) for bayshore locations along the San
Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay Saturday evening
through Thursday morning.

